When a page’s Notification is clicked, Ladybird fires a cancelable
click event with transient activation, and brings the tab into focus
unless the handler canceled it. Firefox does the same: NotificationChild::
RecvNotifyClick() dispatches a cancelable click and, when it isn’t
canceled, calls nsFocusManager::FocusWindow(). Blink’s
Notification::OnClick() and WebKit’s Notification::dispatchClickEvent()
fire the event under a window-focus-allowed scope and leave the focus
to the page’s own focus() call.

A shown notification outlives its page, and a click on one whose page
is gone opens the origin in a new foreground tab. That’s Chromium’s
rule, in NonPersistentNotificationHandler::DidDispatchClickEvent():
when the click event can’t be delivered, it navigates a new tab to the
origin. Firefox instead closes the notification when the page freezes
or goes away (NotificationChild::FrozenCallback()), and WebKit drops the
mapping in Notification::stop() while the platform keeps showing it.

Every notification is attributed to the origin the browser knows the
page by, with the scheme omitted for http(s). Chromium formats the
origin the same way, as the macOS subtitle in
CreateMacNotificationContext() and in the FDO body in
NotificationPlatformBridgeLinuxImpl; Firefox shows a “via host”
subtitle in MacOSNotificationCenter.mm and alert.js, with a “Disable
notifications from host” action besides. Same origin and same tag
replace the earlier notification in all three engines; Ladybird adds a
cap of eight live notifications per origin, which none of them has as a
fixed number (Chromium relies on the platform’s queue limits).

Non-persistent notifications carry no actions in Chromium (checked in
NonPersistentNotificationHandler::OnClick()) and in Firefox, and WebKit
doesn’t implement actions at all; Ladybird’s constructor rejects them
as the spec says, and maxActions reports 2, Chromium’s
kNotificationMaxActions and Firefox’s kMaxActions.
